#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9017b4e-ddb3-56f6-ba30-b494a7e4e09f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee00be0e-f6a2-5269-a36d-1eeaf1b32344" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product of Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a22cfc-9a78-57ab-93a4-3c3255c34bf7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of an Orthogonal Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135177 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a square $n\\times n$ matrix $Q$ with columns $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n{:}$\n\n$$\nQ = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & \\cdots  & {\\mathbf{q}}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $Q$ is said to be **orthogonal** if its columns $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\dots, \\mathbf{q}_n$ form an *orthonormal* set. In other words, \n\n- the columns of $Q$ must be *mutually orthogonal*, and\n\n- they must all be *unit* vectors.\n\nFor instance, the matrix \n$$\nQ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{black}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}} & {\\color{black}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\ {\\color{black}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}}} & {\\color{black}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \nis orthogonal since its columns \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_1}={\\color{black}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}}, \n\\qquad\n{\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_2}={\\color{black}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}}\n$$\n\nform an orthonormal set $\\left\\{ {\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_1}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_2} \\right\\}.$ To see this, note the following:\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf q_1$ and $\\mathbf q_2$ are orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Both $\\mathbf q_1$ and $\\mathbf q_2$ are unit vectors, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{q}_{1}|{|}^{2} & ={q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{q}_{2}|{|}^{2} & ={q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e985a62-c62b-5e6d-8c21-8c92fc9d0eca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8095 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135178 :tutorial/content "Orthogonal matrices also have the following important properties:\n\n- $\\det(Q)=\\pm1$ (the determinant of an orthogonal matrix is either $1$ or $-1$)\n\n- If $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ then $| \\lambda |= 1 .$\n\n**Watch out!** These properties are necessary *but not sufficient* conditions:\n\n- Every orthogonal matrix has determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with determinant $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal!\n\n- Similarly, every orthogonal matrix has eigenvalues $1$ or $-1$ only, but not every matrix with eigenvalues $1$ or $-1$ is orthogonal." :tutorial/id #uuid "40e97f72-22ae-5b05-b248-5575125c1f7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8096 :tutorial/title "Properties of Orthogonal Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135179 :tutorial/content "Recall the following theorem:\n\n*A matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if*\n\n$$\nQ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{Q^T}} = {\\color{blue}{Q^T}} \\cdot Q = I.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that for $Q$ to be invertible, there must exist a matrix $Q^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nQ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{Q^{-1}}} = {\\color{blue}{Q^{-1}}} \\cdot Q = I.\n$$\n\nBy comparing the two equations above, we can deduce the following:\n\n- Every orthogonal matrix $Q$ is invertible, and\n\n- the inverse of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ is ${\\color{blue}{Q^T}},$ that is\n\n$$\nQ^{-1} = Q^T.\n$$\n\nThese properties explain why orthogonal matrices are desirable. We can always find the inverse of an orthogonal matrix, and we can quickly compute the inverse by simply transposing the original matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "90b27dd6-cc1a-5dce-8437-3591d747d64f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8097 :tutorial/title "The Inverse of an Orthogonal Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135182 :tutorial/content "Checking that the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal set is a fair amount of work. Thankfully, we have the following theorem to make our lives easier: \n\n*A matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if*\n\n$$\nQ \\cdot Q^T = Q^T \\cdot Q = I\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $Q^T$ denotes the transpose of $Q.$  It's the matrix we get when we interchange the rows and columns of $Q.$\n\n- To verify that a matrix is orthogonal in practice, we only need to check that $Q \\, Q^T = I.$\n\nLet's see an example of applying this theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "b840b2b3-6615-5d30-96ea-42b4e51360f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8173 :tutorial/title "A Straightforward Test of Orthogonality"}]}
